  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": null,
      "what": "The study integrated 1,725 viral genomes linked to patient residential addresses with urban mobility data to reconstruct the spatiotemporal spread of SARS-CoV-2 across epidemic waves three to five in Hong Kong. Using Bayesian phylodynamics, it identified that these waves originated in the high-density districts of Wong Tai Sin, Yau Tsim Mong, and Kwai Tsing, which then acted as the primary exporters of regional transmission. Statistical modeling revealed that population density and Mass Transit Railway (MTR) usage were consistent predictors of transmission rates between districts.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that combining pathogen genomic surveillance with intra-city transit and demographic data can pinpoint specific neighborhood-level transmission hotspots and pathways, providing a framework for targeted public health interventions in high-density urban environments."
